			<outline text="readme.md">
				<outline text="# Hello Frontier">
					<outline text="I'm working on a new version of Frontier, a complete rewrite, running in Electron, a project that could only be approached with the new AI tools, in this case Claude Code. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="To start it's just for sharing bits of code and documenting decisions I had to make while we're going along. And to give people with Frontier expertise a way to be involved and help Frontier emerge from its holiday, to return to party with us, same as it ever was. There will be a rule in this repo -- people only.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Dave Winer, August 2026">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Background">
					<outline text="Frontier is a lot bigger than I remembered, because I only use a fraction of it when I'm working on my websites and apps. That's how I've been using it since 2013 or so. Writing, documenting and supporting my online projects.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Now we're trying to get Frontier itself running in this mode. We can do almost everything, but some parts will be hard, and probably a few things that worked on the Mac up till the late teens, won't work there either, mostly having to do with background processes. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### How this works">
					<outline text="I'm going to raise issues in the Issues section here, outlining a decision I have to make, usually about how much of Frontier we should:">
						<outline text="1. Do now.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="2. Support later, maybe.">
						</outline>
						<outline text="3. Won't support. ">
						</outline>
					</outline>
					<outline text="Things like mac-only data types, that the mac of today probably doesn't support are in category 3.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="Some tcp verbs have already been ported, others need to be, and still others aren't generally used at the JS and above layer unless they're doing something really specific with the OS.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="There also will be features we will add, for example, we have much better feed reading and building functionality. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="And there are implementations that have changed, and possibly no longer work the same way as the original app worked. Those are bugs, and we're fixing them as they are discovered. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### Policy about who writes">
					<outline text="People are starting to paste huge documents from ChatGPT or Claude into issues sections of repos, and I want to stamp that out right now, if not everywhere, here. It's realllly rude to have a bot speak for you, and then to not speak to us as if we're all busy people who need you to get to the point. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="That said, Claude is much better at writing up the issues than I am. But it's posts will be carefully identitifed, and probably in a special folder on the repo, pointed to by me. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="We're all building with these tools now, and they're fantastic, but this is a place for humans to communicate with humans. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="### What is Frontier?">
					<outline text="When we want to know what Frontier does, we refer to the Frontier source code [archived](https://github.com/tedchoward/Frontier) by Ted C. Howard, archived in November 2011. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/secondsSinceFractional.md">
				<outline text="# date.secondsSinceFractional and clock.milliseconds">
					<outline text="New verbs. date.secondsSince, a great utility for log messages and such, in Frontier has always been expressed in whole numbers. But there are times when you need more precision. The difference here is that the &lt;i>when&lt;/i> value must come from clock.milliseconds, where you used clock.now for date.secondsSince. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="clock.milliseconds is also a new kernel-implemented verb.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@date.secondsSinceFractional)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/opConsole.md">
				<outline text="# op.console.start and op.console.log">
					<outline text="After pending 13 years working in JavaScript, I have come to love console.log. Sometimes debugging isn't an option. And sometimes you want every call to a function to announce itself, so you can click to start the debugger. So I wanted this in Frontier, and quickly put this together. A console logging function that works in an outline. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@op.console.start)%>">
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@op.console.log)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/helloFrontier.md">
				<outline text="# workspace.userlandSamples.helloFrontier">
					<outline text="The first object that we're distributing through rootupdates.">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@workspace.userlandSamples.helloFrontier)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/stringAddressToString.md">
				<outline text="# string.addressToString">
					<outline text="I noticed that the names of objects in the Window menu were much longer than they needed to be. I asked if we had a way to shorten them, and we did not. Then I looked in the pre-Atlantis Frontier, and it had the same problem, which told me I've been using it this way for &lt;i>decades&lt;/i> or so it seems. So together we figure out where this fit -- there was even a string.stringToAddress verb. ">
					</outline>
					<outline text="9/14/26; 10:51:38 AM by DW">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@system.verbs.builtins.string.addressToString)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/timing.md">
				<outline text="# workspace.timing">
					<outline text="9/17/26 by DW:  Convert to factional seconds. Earlier versions of Frontier couldn't do fractional seconds in date.secondsSince. Now we have two new verbs, clock.milliseconds and date.secondsSinceFractional.">
					</outline>
					<outline text="9/15/26 by DW: The last time we ran this script was in August, now it takes approx twice the time for each call. That's why we have benchmark scripts like this. ;-)">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@workspace.timing)%>">
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@workspace.doVeryLittle)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
			<outline text="examples/twitterNewPost.md">
				<outline text="# twitter.newPost">
					<outline text="Was having a conversation with friends about the capability of glue scripts. This is an example of a script that was started in 2007, about a year after Twitter started up. No doubt this API is no longer supported, but it gives you an idea what's possible using HTTP. It was being maintained through April, 2011. ">
					</outline>
				</outline>
				<outline text="&lt;%exampleUsertalkCode (@twitter.newpost)%>">
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</outline>
		<outline text="build script">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildHelloFrontier ()">
			</outline>
		</outline>
